<%# Travel has no tools, so this partial is the whole page — hence rather
    more of it than the other section hubs carry. The map's constraints
    (own basemap, no geocoder, owner-only) are the interesting part and are
    the privacy story in miniature. %>

<section class="mt-12 max-w-3xl space-y-5">
  <p>
    A trip isn't one kind of thing. It's a flight, a passport, an insurance
    policy, a budget line, the friend whose spare room you used, a plan, and
    four hundred photographs. Every travel app has to pick one of those to
    be about, and ends up being about that one.
  </p>
  <p>
    Because everything in this app can belong to several parts of your life
    at once, Travel doesn't have to pick. The flight is travel and money.
    The friend is travel and people. Nothing gets filed away from where it
    also belongs.
  </p>
</section>

<%= about_screenshot("world_map") %>

<section class="mt-14">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">The map of where you've been</h2>
  <div class="mt-6 grid grid-cols-1 gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "Drawn from your own photos") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        Most photographs quietly record where they were taken. Yours are
        already in this app, so the map of everywhere you've been draws
        itself — no check-ins, no app that tracked you, nothing you had to
        remember to do at the time.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "No tiles, no lookups") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        The world map is a file inside the app, drawn from public-domain
        coastline data. Nothing is fetched while you pan it, so no map
        service ever receives a request that says where your photographs
        were taken. That is not a setting — there is simply nowhere for it
        to go.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "Trips it noticed") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        A cluster of photographs somewhere you don't live, over a few days,
        is a trip. The app can propose them and you keep the ones that were
        real — a decade of travel recovered from a library you already had.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "Yours only") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        Anything derived from photo coordinates stays with the person whose
        library it came from, even inside a trip shared with the household.
        Where you have been is a strong claim about you, and it is not
        something sharing a holiday hands over.
      </p>
    <% end %>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="mt-14">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">And where you're going</h2>
  <div class="mt-6 grid grid-cols-1 gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "Booked, or only discussed") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        The distinction that actually matters when a trip is coming
        together. Each stay and each leg is either genuinely booked or still
        a conversation, and the difference is visible at a glance instead of
        living in somebody's head.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "Where will we be in March?") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        An outlook across the coming months, and a band on the calendar
        beside everything else. Built for households who move often enough
        that this is a real question with a real answer.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "Somewhere to sleep, every night") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        Stays and travel legs laid end to end, so the gap between the
        Tuesday checkout and the Thursday flight is visible now rather than
        on the Tuesday.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "The people coming with you") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        A trip can be shared with the household members who are on it, so
        everyone sees the same plan — while each person's photographs, and
        anything drawn from them, stay their own.
      </p>
    <% end %>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="mt-14 max-w-3xl">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">The same record, before and after</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    A trip you planned in February is the trip the photographs attach to in
    July. It doesn't graduate from one feature to another, or get retyped
    into a scrapbook — the thing you used to keep track of the booking is
    the thing that ends up holding the memory of it.
  </p>
</section>
